Using the ACS couples dataset to examine which factors are associated with couples violating the “half your age plus seven” dating guideline. 
I filtered the dataset to include heterosexual couples where the male partner was older than the female partner. 
I then calculated the minimum age permitted under the guideline by dividing the male partner's age by two and adding seven.

I created a binary variable called violation, assigning a value of 1 when the female partner was younger than the guideline's minimum age and 0 otherwise. 
My final estimation sample included 272,091 couples, of which 3,842 (1.41%) violated the guideline.

OLS-
The OLS regression examined the relationship between the dating rule and household income, geographic region, employment status, and usual weekly hours worked.
I found that higher male householder income was associated with a lower probability of violating the dating rule. 
The relationship was statistically significant (p < 0.001). Specifically, a $10,000 increase in income was associated with a 0.0245-percentage-point decrease in the predicted probability of violation.
I also found that regions 31, 41, and 42 had significantly higher probabilities of violation compared with the reference region. 
Employment status was statistically significant, although the unusually large coefficients suggested that I should investigate how the reference category affected the results. 
Hours worked had a small negative relationship with violations and was marginally significant (p = 0.045).
The model had an R-squared of 0.0886, indicating that the included variables explained approximately 8.9% of the variation in guideline violations.

Logit-
Compared to the logistic regression, this provided a more appropriate framework for estimating the probability of a violation as the dependent variable was binary.
The regression generally supported my OLS findings. 
Higher income remained statistically significant and negatively associated with violations. 
A $10,000 increase in income was associated with approximately 2.9% lower odds of violating the guideline.
Regions 31, 41, and 42 also remained statistically significant.
However, I noticed some differences between the models. Hours worked was no longer statistically significant at the 5% level (p = 0.076).
Employment status also became statistically insignificant, but the logistic regression produced unusually large standard errors. 

By comparing OLS and logistic regression, both models produced similar conclusions about the significant of the variables.
The logistic regression identified more actual violations, while OLS produced fewer false positives and higher overall accuracy.
Overall, both models identified income and certain geographic regions as consistent correlates of guideline violations.
